Algorithm
Set two pointers: l = 0 (left) and r = len(height) - 1 (right).
Set res = 0 to store the maximum area found.
Loop until l meets r
Calculate width = r - l.
Calculate container_height = min(height[l], height[r]).The area is limited by the shorter line.If we move the taller line inward, width decreases and height stays the same or decreases, so area cannot improve.Moving the shorter line might find a taller height, which could increase the area despite a smaller width.
Calculate area = width * container_height.
Update res = max(res, area).
If height[l] < height[r], increment l (l += 1).
Else, decrement r (r -= 1).
Code
class Solution:

def maxArea(self, height: List[int]) -> int:
    res=0
    l,r=0,len(height)-1
    while l<r:
        area=(r-l)*min(height[l],height[r])
        res=max(area,res)

        if height[l]<height[r]:
            l+=1
        else:
            r-=1
    return res
Complexity
Time Complexity:

We start with two pointers at opposite ends.

In each iteration, one pointer moves inward by 1 (either l increases or r decreases).

This means each pointer moves at most n-1 steps.

Therefore, the loop runs O(n) times.

Inside the loop, all operations (min, subtraction, multiplication, comparison) are O(1).

Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity:

We only use a few extra variables: l, r, res, area, width, container_height.

These are constant in size and do not grow with input.

No extra arrays or data structures are used.

Space Complexity: O(1) Continue until l >= r.

Return res.
